1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,879
It's a Saturday morning in nineteen eighty-four.

2
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:04,275
You've got twenty dollars from your paper

3
00:00:04,275 --> 00:00:04,897
route in your pocket,

4
00:00:04,897 --> 00:00:05,906
your hair is feathered just right,

5
00:00:05,906 --> 00:00:06,855
and your mom is dropping you and

6
00:00:06,855 --> 00:00:07,981
your friends off at the main entrance.

7
00:00:08,161 --> 00:00:09,772
She'll pick you up at four,

8
00:00:09,772 --> 00:00:10,727
by the fountain.

9
00:00:10,907 --> 00:00:12,995
You walk through the glass doors,

10
00:00:12,995 --> 00:00:14,007
and there it is.

11
00:00:14,187 --> 00:00:14,814
The mall.

12
00:00:15,163 --> 00:00:16,949
In the nineteen eighties,

13
00:00:16,949 --> 00:00:19,448
the shopping mall was the center of

14
00:00:19,448 --> 00:00:22,304
American life, especially for teenagers.

15
00:00:22,484 --> 00:00:24,370
It was where you shopped,

16
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:26,181
ate, played video games,

17
00:00:26,181 --> 00:00:28,974
saw movies, met friends and maybe met

18
00:00:28,974 --> 00:00:30,181
someone special.

19
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:31,366
In those years,

20
00:00:31,366 --> 00:00:33,978
America built new malls at a remarkable

21
00:00:33,978 --> 00:00:35,786
pace, and by the end of the

22
00:00:35,786 --> 00:00:38,198
decade there were thousands of them.

23
00:00:38,547 --> 00:00:40,740
So let's spend a Saturday at the

24
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,727
mall in nineteen eighty-four.

25
00:00:42,907 --> 00:00:45,078
From opening time to closing time.

26
00:00:45,258 --> 00:00:47,501
The stores, the food court,

27
00:00:47,501 --> 00:00:50,576
the arcade, the music and the people.

28
00:00:50,756 --> 00:00:51,758
If you were there,

29
00:00:51,758 --> 00:00:54,096
you might just hear the fountain splashing

30
00:00:54,096 --> 00:00:55,376
and smell the Cinnabon.

31
00:00:55,726 --> 00:00:58,281
The mall had been growing for decades.

32
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:01,000
The first fully enclosed mall,

33
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,200
Southdale Center in Edina,

34
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:06,924
Minnesota, had opened in nineteen fifty-six.

35
00:01:07,104 --> 00:01:09,416
Through the sixties and seventies,

36
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:11,796
malls spread to every suburb in the

37
00:01:11,796 --> 00:01:12,340
country.

38
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:13,570
By the eighties,

39
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:16,062
they had pulled the shoppers away from

40
00:01:16,062 --> 00:01:18,685
downtown Main Streets almost everywhere.

41
00:01:18,865 --> 00:01:20,866
The mall had become the new Main

42
00:01:20,866 --> 00:01:21,303
Street.

43
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:22,709
At ten o'clock,

44
00:01:22,709 --> 00:01:25,312
the metal gates rolled up on hundreds

45
00:01:25,312 --> 00:01:26,367
of storefronts.

46
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:28,776
The mall was clean and quiet.

47
00:01:28,956 --> 00:01:31,435
The first people inside were the mall

48
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:34,249
walkers, older men and women in tracksuits

49
00:01:34,249 --> 00:01:35,522
and white sneakers,

50
00:01:35,522 --> 00:01:38,000
walking laps in the air conditioning.

51
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:40,653
Many malls opened early just for them.

52
00:01:41,003 --> 00:01:43,749
The malls of the eighties were bright

53
00:01:43,749 --> 00:01:45,752
and modern, with skylights,

54
00:01:45,752 --> 00:01:48,127
glass elevators, brass railings,

55
00:01:48,127 --> 00:01:50,872
potted palm trees and huge fountains.

56
00:01:51,052 --> 00:01:53,307
Some had ice skating rinks,

57
00:01:53,307 --> 00:01:56,648
carousels or even small amusement parks.

58
00:01:56,828 --> 00:01:59,987
Shoppers threw coins into the fountains and

59
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:00,868
made wishes.

60
00:02:01,048 --> 00:02:03,837
Some malls collected thousands of dollars a

61
00:02:03,837 --> 00:02:05,588
year from them for charity.

62
00:02:05,938 --> 00:02:08,008
At each end of the mall stood

63
00:02:08,008 --> 00:02:09,294
the anchor stores,

64
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:11,150
the big department stores.

65
00:02:11,330 --> 00:02:12,724
Sears, J.

66
00:02:12,904 --> 00:02:13,124
C.

67
00:02:13,304 --> 00:02:15,175
Penney, Montgomery Ward,

68
00:02:15,175 --> 00:02:18,372
Macy's or a regional chain like Hudson's,

69
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:20,711
Marshall Field's or Bullock's.

70
00:02:20,891 --> 00:02:23,069
Parents shopped there for clothes,

71
00:02:23,069 --> 00:02:24,479
appliances and towels.

72
00:02:24,659 --> 00:02:27,007
Kids went there to use the restroom

73
00:02:27,007 --> 00:02:28,885
and look at the electronics.

74
00:02:29,235 --> 00:02:31,243
Getting there was half the adventure.

75
00:02:31,423 --> 00:02:34,653
Teenagers without a license begged rides from

76
00:02:34,653 --> 00:02:36,520
parents or older siblings.

77
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:37,752
In some towns,

78
00:02:37,752 --> 00:02:40,082
a city bus stopped right at the

79
00:02:40,082 --> 00:02:41,135
mall entrance.

80
00:02:41,315 --> 00:02:43,608
Kids rode their bikes for miles and

81
00:02:43,608 --> 00:02:46,098
locked them to the rack outside Sears.

82
00:02:46,278 --> 00:02:48,122
And once you got your license,

83
00:02:48,122 --> 00:02:50,273
driving your friends to the mall on

84
00:02:50,273 --> 00:02:52,608
Saturday was the whole point of having

85
00:02:52,608 --> 00:02:52,977
a car.

86
00:02:53,328 --> 00:02:55,614
In between were the smaller stores,

87
00:02:55,614 --> 00:02:56,790
and for teenagers,

88
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:58,749
they were the real attraction.

89
00:02:58,929 --> 00:03:00,940
There was the record store,

90
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:03,323
like Sam Goody or Camelot Music,

91
00:03:03,323 --> 00:03:06,226
with racks of vinyl albums and cassette

92
00:03:06,226 --> 00:03:06,673
tapes.

93
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:08,671
In nineteen eighty-four,

94
00:03:08,671 --> 00:03:12,231
everyone was buying Michael Jackson's Thriller,

95
00:03:12,231 --> 00:03:13,821
Prince's Purple Rain,

96
00:03:13,821 --> 00:03:16,397
Bruce Springsteen's Born in the U.

97
00:03:16,577 --> 00:03:16,983
S.

98
00:03:17,163 --> 00:03:17,418
A.

99
00:03:17,598 --> 00:03:18,527
and Madonna.

100
00:03:18,878 --> 00:03:20,646
There was the bookstore,

101
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:20,793
B.

102
00:03:20,973 --> 00:03:22,650
Dalton or Waldenbooks,

103
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:25,776
where kids read comic books and magazines

104
00:03:25,776 --> 00:03:28,825
without buying them until a clerk shooed

105
00:03:28,825 --> 00:03:29,588
them away.

106
00:03:29,768 --> 00:03:31,537
There was Radio Shack,

107
00:03:31,537 --> 00:03:33,066
with its batteries,

108
00:03:33,066 --> 00:03:36,525
walkie-talkies and the new TRS-80 computer.

109
00:03:36,705 --> 00:03:38,868
And there was Spencer Gifts,

110
00:03:38,868 --> 00:03:40,568
with its black lights,

111
00:03:40,568 --> 00:03:43,195
lava lamps, gag gifts and posters.

112
00:03:43,545 --> 00:03:46,439
For clothes, teenagers went to The Limited,

113
00:03:46,439 --> 00:03:48,256
Chess King, Merry-Go-Round,

114
00:03:48,256 --> 00:03:49,872
County Seat and The Gap.

115
00:03:50,052 --> 00:03:54,344
The fashion of eighty-four meant parachute pants,

116
00:03:54,344 --> 00:03:56,184
Members Only jackets,

117
00:03:56,184 --> 00:03:58,987
Swatch watches, jelly bracelets,

118
00:03:58,987 --> 00:04:01,790
leg warmers and acid-wash jeans.

119
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:04,453
Girls wore big bangs held up with

120
00:04:04,453 --> 00:04:05,882
Aqua Net hairspray.

121
00:04:06,062 --> 00:04:08,375
Boys wore Izod shirts with the collar

122
00:04:08,375 --> 00:04:09,000
popped up.

123
00:04:09,350 --> 00:04:11,068
Then there was the shoe store.

124
00:04:11,248 --> 00:04:14,507
Nike Air Jordans wouldn't arrive until the

125
00:04:14,507 --> 00:04:17,921
following year, but in nineteen eighty-four,

126
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:19,240
kids wanted Nike,

127
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:22,033
Reebok, Converse high-tops and Vans.

128
00:04:22,213 --> 00:04:24,965
Girls wanted Keds and jelly shoes.

129
00:04:25,145 --> 00:04:26,696
And at the Foot Locker,

130
00:04:26,696 --> 00:04:29,596
the clerks wore referee shirts and measured

131
00:04:29,596 --> 00:04:32,227
your feet with a metal Brannock device,

132
00:04:32,227 --> 00:04:34,520
just like the shoe stores of their

133
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:35,397
parents' day.

134
00:04:35,747 --> 00:04:38,069
And there was the toy store,

135
00:04:38,069 --> 00:04:39,644
KB Toys or Kay-Bee,

136
00:04:39,644 --> 00:04:41,883
packed with He-Man figures,

137
00:04:41,883 --> 00:04:45,449
Transformers, which arrived that very year,

138
00:04:45,449 --> 00:04:49,679
Care Bears, Strawberry Shortcake and Rubik's Cubes.

139
00:04:49,859 --> 00:04:52,450
And everyone was looking for a Cabbage

140
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:53,133
Patch Kid.

141
00:04:53,313 --> 00:04:54,451
The year before,

142
00:04:54,451 --> 00:04:57,153
at Christmas of nineteen eighty-three,

143
00:04:57,153 --> 00:05:00,069
parents had literally fought in stores to

144
00:05:00,069 --> 00:05:00,638
get one.

145
00:05:00,989 --> 00:05:03,158
And there was the electronics store,

146
00:05:03,158 --> 00:05:06,050
where crowds gathered around the newest gadgets.

147
00:05:06,230 --> 00:05:07,366
The Sony Walkman,

148
00:05:07,366 --> 00:05:10,039
which let you carry your music anywhere.

149
00:05:10,219 --> 00:05:13,086
Boom boxes the size of suitcases.

150
00:05:13,266 --> 00:05:16,703
Atari and ColecoVision game consoles.

151
00:05:16,883 --> 00:05:19,111
And at the very end of January

152
00:05:19,111 --> 00:05:23,716
nineteen eighty-four, Apple introduced the Macintosh computer,

153
00:05:23,716 --> 00:05:26,538
with a famous television ad during the

154
00:05:26,538 --> 00:05:27,355
Super Bowl.

155
00:05:27,705 --> 00:05:28,836
For many kids,

156
00:05:28,836 --> 00:05:31,181
the best part of the mall was

157
00:05:31,181 --> 00:05:32,070
the arcade.

158
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:34,607
Aladdin's Castle, Tilt,

159
00:05:34,607 --> 00:05:35,837
or Time Out.

160
00:05:36,017 --> 00:05:38,785
Dark rooms full of flashing lights and

161
00:05:38,785 --> 00:05:41,843
electronic sounds, where every game cost a

162
00:05:41,843 --> 00:05:42,426
quarter.

163
00:05:42,606 --> 00:05:45,200
The change machine on the wall turned

164
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:47,513
your dollar bills into a stack of

165
00:05:47,513 --> 00:05:50,036
quarters, and they disappeared fast.

166
00:05:50,387 --> 00:05:53,231
The games of nineteen eighty-four were legends.

167
00:05:53,411 --> 00:05:55,060
Pac-Man, Ms.

168
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,419
Pac-Man, Donkey Kong,

169
00:05:57,419 --> 00:06:02,089
Galaga, Centipede, Frogger and Dragon's Lair.

170
00:06:02,269 --> 00:06:04,313
The best players drew a crowd.

171
00:06:04,493 --> 00:06:06,398
They put their initials on the high

172
00:06:06,398 --> 00:06:07,105
score screen.

173
00:06:07,285 --> 00:06:09,502
And a kid who could play Galaga

174
00:06:09,502 --> 00:06:11,647
for an hour on one quarter was

175
00:06:11,647 --> 00:06:13,578
famous at school on Monday.

176
00:06:13,928 --> 00:06:16,410
Parents left younger kids at the arcade

177
00:06:16,410 --> 00:06:17,620
while they shopped.

178
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,307
Older kids hung out there for hours.

179
00:06:20,487 --> 00:06:21,320
It was loud,

180
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,681
dark and a little bit rough around

181
00:06:23,681 --> 00:06:26,111
the edges, and that was exactly why

182
00:06:26,111 --> 00:06:27,430
teenagers loved it.

183
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:29,872
Next door, there might be a photo

184
00:06:29,872 --> 00:06:30,252
booth.

185
00:06:30,432 --> 00:06:32,318
Four pictures for a dollar,

186
00:06:32,318 --> 00:06:34,553
printed in a strip a few minutes

187
00:06:34,553 --> 00:06:34,972
later.

188
00:06:35,152 --> 00:06:37,837
Friends crammed into the tiny booth,

189
00:06:37,837 --> 00:06:39,105
made silly faces,

190
00:06:39,105 --> 00:06:40,597
and split the strip.

191
00:06:40,777 --> 00:06:43,640
Many people still have those little strips

192
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:46,367
tucked into old yearbooks and shoeboxes,

193
00:06:46,367 --> 00:06:48,617
the only photos they have of that

194
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:49,299
afternoon.

195
00:06:49,649 --> 00:06:51,618
By noon, everyone was hungry,

196
00:06:51,618 --> 00:06:54,200
and everyone headed to the food court.

197
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:56,468
The food court was a new idea

198
00:06:56,468 --> 00:06:58,629
in the seventies and eighties.

199
00:06:58,809 --> 00:07:01,163
A big open area full of tables,

200
00:07:01,163 --> 00:07:04,505
surrounded by counters selling every kind of

201
00:07:04,505 --> 00:07:05,264
fast food.

202
00:07:05,444 --> 00:07:08,284
Pizza, tacos, Chinese food,

203
00:07:08,284 --> 00:07:10,808
hot dogs and hamburgers,

204
00:07:10,808 --> 00:07:12,596
all in one place.

205
00:07:12,946 --> 00:07:14,649
There was Orange Julius,

206
00:07:14,649 --> 00:07:16,707
with its frothy orange drink.

207
00:07:16,887 --> 00:07:18,358
Hot Dog on a Stick,

208
00:07:18,358 --> 00:07:21,841
where the employees wore those famous striped

209
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:23,621
uniforms and tall hats.

210
00:07:23,801 --> 00:07:25,519
Sbarro for pizza.

211
00:07:25,699 --> 00:07:28,763
Chick-fil-A, which started in a mall in

212
00:07:28,763 --> 00:07:29,391
Atlanta.

213
00:07:29,571 --> 00:07:31,781
And the Great American Cookie Company,

214
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:32,886
with giant cookies,

215
00:07:32,886 --> 00:07:35,211
and the smell of fresh pretzels drifting

216
00:07:35,211 --> 00:07:36,549
through the whole mall.

217
00:07:36,899 --> 00:07:39,936
Teenagers sat at the food court for

218
00:07:39,936 --> 00:07:42,019
hours, nursing one soda.

219
00:07:42,199 --> 00:07:43,934
They gossiped, flirted,

220
00:07:43,934 --> 00:07:46,877
made plans, and watched everyone go by.

221
00:07:47,057 --> 00:07:48,692
For a generation of kids,

222
00:07:48,692 --> 00:07:50,783
the food court was what the soda

223
00:07:50,783 --> 00:07:53,071
fountain had been to their parents.

224
00:07:53,251 --> 00:07:55,004
The place where everyone met.

225
00:07:55,355 --> 00:07:57,839
And the sweet smells were everywhere.

226
00:07:58,019 --> 00:08:00,565
Cinnabon opened its first store in a

227
00:08:00,565 --> 00:08:03,535
mall near Seattle in nineteen eighty-five,

228
00:08:03,535 --> 00:08:06,010
and the smell of its cinnamon rolls

229
00:08:06,010 --> 00:08:08,909
would soon fill malls across the country.

230
00:08:09,089 --> 00:08:12,207
There were candy stores selling jelly beans

231
00:08:12,207 --> 00:08:13,150
by the pound,

232
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:15,471
made famous by President Reagan,

233
00:08:15,471 --> 00:08:17,283
who kept a jar of them on

234
00:08:17,283 --> 00:08:17,936
his desk.

235
00:08:18,286 --> 00:08:20,320
Hanging out at the mall was a

236
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:21,513
national pastime.

237
00:08:21,693 --> 00:08:23,133
Parents felt it was safe.

238
00:08:23,313 --> 00:08:24,327
It was indoors,

239
00:08:24,327 --> 00:08:26,154
there were security guards,

240
00:08:26,154 --> 00:08:28,724
and nothing bad was supposed to happen

241
00:08:28,724 --> 00:08:29,536
at the mall.

242
00:08:29,716 --> 00:08:31,926
So kids were dropped off for whole

243
00:08:31,926 --> 00:08:32,641
afternoons.

244
00:08:32,821 --> 00:08:33,962
They walked laps,

245
00:08:33,962 --> 00:08:37,117
window shopped, and went nowhere in particular.

246
00:08:37,467 --> 00:08:39,417
Kids called them mall rats.

247
00:08:39,597 --> 00:08:41,370
They had no money to spend,

248
00:08:41,370 --> 00:08:43,076
but they knew every store,

249
00:08:43,076 --> 00:08:44,914
every clerk and every bench.

250
00:08:45,094 --> 00:08:47,288
They knew which store had the cutest

251
00:08:47,288 --> 00:08:50,030
employees and which security guard would kick

252
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:52,223
you out for sitting on the fountain.

253
00:08:52,403 --> 00:08:54,075
The mall was their neighborhood.

254
00:08:54,425 --> 00:08:56,937
Teenagers had their first jobs at the

255
00:08:56,937 --> 00:08:57,548
mall too.

256
00:08:57,728 --> 00:08:59,101
Scooping ice cream,

257
00:08:59,101 --> 00:09:01,125
folding sweaters at the Gap,

258
00:09:01,125 --> 00:09:03,945
selling shoes at Foot Locker or working

259
00:09:03,945 --> 00:09:05,897
the counter at Hot Dog on a

260
00:09:05,897 --> 00:09:06,331
Stick.

261
00:09:06,511 --> 00:09:10,170
A sixteen-year-old in nineteen eighty-four earned the

262
00:09:10,170 --> 00:09:13,070
minimum wage of three thirty-five an hour.

263
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:15,085
And you got an employee discount.

264
00:09:15,435 --> 00:09:17,559
And the mall had its own soundtrack.

265
00:09:17,739 --> 00:09:20,637
Soft music played from speakers in the

266
00:09:20,637 --> 00:09:24,450
ceiling, easy-listening versions of popular songs.

267
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:27,083
Each store played its own music too,

268
00:09:27,083 --> 00:09:29,946
so walking down the corridor meant hearing

269
00:09:29,946 --> 00:09:31,445
a dozen songs at once.

270
00:09:31,625 --> 00:09:33,390
The splash of the fountain.

271
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:35,717
The beep of the cash registers.

272
00:09:35,897 --> 00:09:38,380
The squeak of sneakers on the shiny

273
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:39,160
tile floor.

274
00:09:39,510 --> 00:09:40,587
In the afternoon,

275
00:09:40,587 --> 00:09:41,727
there was a movie.

276
00:09:41,907 --> 00:09:44,676
Many malls had a movie theater with

277
00:09:44,676 --> 00:09:46,180
two to six screens.

278
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:49,076
In the summer of nineteen eighty-four,

279
00:09:49,076 --> 00:09:51,363
it might have been Ghostbusters,

280
00:09:51,363 --> 00:09:54,008
Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom,

281
00:09:54,008 --> 00:09:55,938
Gremlins or The Karate Kid.

282
00:09:56,118 --> 00:09:59,155
Tickets cost about three dollars and fifty

283
00:09:59,155 --> 00:09:59,589
cents.

284
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,285
Families came in the afternoon too.

285
00:10:02,465 --> 00:10:04,079
Mothers with strollers.

286
00:10:04,259 --> 00:10:07,707
Fathers looking at power tools at Sears.

287
00:10:07,887 --> 00:10:10,545
Grandparents resting on the benches.

288
00:10:10,725 --> 00:10:14,100
Little kids riding the coin-operated horses and

289
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,033
rocket ships.

290
00:10:15,213 --> 00:10:17,602
On weekends, there might be a car

291
00:10:17,602 --> 00:10:19,413
show in the center court,

292
00:10:19,413 --> 00:10:20,354
a craft fair,

293
00:10:20,354 --> 00:10:22,237
or a school choir singing.

294
00:10:22,587 --> 00:10:23,849
And at Christmas,

295
00:10:23,849 --> 00:10:25,408
the mall was magical.

296
00:10:25,588 --> 00:10:28,258
Giant decorations hung from the ceiling.

297
00:10:28,438 --> 00:10:30,258
Santa sat on a throne in the

298
00:10:30,258 --> 00:10:32,402
center court, and the line to see

299
00:10:32,402 --> 00:10:34,092
him stretched for an hour.

300
00:10:34,272 --> 00:10:37,186
Shoppers carried armloads of bags.

301
00:10:37,366 --> 00:10:39,247
And the sound of carols mixed with

302
00:10:39,247 --> 00:10:40,907
the splashing of the fountain.

303
00:10:41,258 --> 00:10:42,342
By four o'clock,

304
00:10:42,342 --> 00:10:43,358
your feet hurt,

305
00:10:43,358 --> 00:10:44,713
your money was gone,

306
00:10:44,713 --> 00:10:46,813
and your mom was waiting by the

307
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:47,423
fountain.

308
00:10:47,603 --> 00:10:49,601
You had a bag from the record

309
00:10:49,601 --> 00:10:51,667
store with one cassette in it,

310
00:10:51,667 --> 00:10:53,803
a Swatch you'd been saving for,

311
00:10:53,803 --> 00:10:55,801
and a story about who you saw

312
00:10:55,801 --> 00:10:57,041
at the food court.

313
00:10:57,221 --> 00:10:58,719
It had been a great day.

314
00:10:59,069 --> 00:11:00,265
At nine o'clock,

315
00:11:00,265 --> 00:11:01,611
the stores closed.

316
00:11:01,791 --> 00:11:03,254
The gates rolled down.

317
00:11:03,434 --> 00:11:04,514
The lights dimmed.

318
00:11:04,694 --> 00:11:07,088
The cleaning crews came out with their

319
00:11:07,088 --> 00:11:08,096
floor polishers.

320
00:11:08,276 --> 00:11:10,357
And in the empty parking lot,

321
00:11:10,357 --> 00:11:12,868
the last cars pulled away under the

322
00:11:12,868 --> 00:11:14,231
tall orange lights.

323
00:11:14,411 --> 00:11:17,024
Tomorrow, it would all start again.

324
00:11:17,374 --> 00:11:19,080
So what happened to the mall?

325
00:11:19,260 --> 00:11:21,684
In the nineties and two thousands,

326
00:11:21,684 --> 00:11:23,324
things began to change.

327
00:11:23,504 --> 00:11:27,269
Big-box stores like Walmart and Target offered

328
00:11:27,269 --> 00:11:28,334
lower prices.

329
00:11:28,514 --> 00:11:31,056
Outlet malls and strip centers grew.

330
00:11:31,236 --> 00:11:32,896
Then came online shopping.

331
00:11:33,076 --> 00:11:35,970
Amazon, founded in nineteen ninety-four,

332
00:11:35,970 --> 00:11:38,790
made it possible to buy almost anything

333
00:11:38,790 --> 00:11:40,309
without leaving home.

334
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:43,051
The anchor stores began to close.

335
00:11:43,231 --> 00:11:44,903
Sears, J.

336
00:11:45,083 --> 00:11:45,304
C.

337
00:11:45,484 --> 00:11:48,932
Penney and Macy's shut hundreds of locations.

338
00:11:49,112 --> 00:11:50,807
When an anchor closed,

339
00:11:50,807 --> 00:11:54,197
the smaller stores lost their customers too.

340
00:11:54,377 --> 00:11:56,989
Many malls fell into decline.

341
00:11:57,169 --> 00:12:00,443
Today, hundreds of American malls are dead

342
00:12:00,443 --> 00:12:03,016
or dying, with empty storefronts,

343
00:12:03,016 --> 00:12:05,900
dry fountains and silent food courts.

344
00:12:06,250 --> 00:12:08,743
Some malls have been torn down and

345
00:12:08,743 --> 00:12:10,576
replaced with apartments,

346
00:12:10,576 --> 00:12:13,216
offices or outdoor shopping centers.

347
00:12:13,396 --> 00:12:15,338
Some have become churches,

348
00:12:15,338 --> 00:12:17,355
schools or medical centers.

349
00:12:17,535 --> 00:12:21,282
Photographers travel the country taking pictures of

350
00:12:21,282 --> 00:12:24,442
abandoned malls, and people watch videos of

351
00:12:24,442 --> 00:12:27,676
them online, feeling a strange nostalgia for

352
00:12:27,676 --> 00:12:30,248
a place that once felt so ordinary.

353
00:12:30,598 --> 00:12:31,863
What did we lose?

354
00:12:32,043 --> 00:12:33,181
Not just stores.

355
00:12:33,361 --> 00:12:35,372
We lost a place where the whole

356
00:12:35,372 --> 00:12:37,643
community gathered, under one roof,

357
00:12:37,643 --> 00:12:39,201
on a Saturday afternoon.

358
00:12:39,381 --> 00:12:42,591
Teenagers, parents, grandparents and toddlers,

359
00:12:42,591 --> 00:12:44,546
all in the same place at the

360
00:12:44,546 --> 00:12:45,244
same time.

361
00:12:45,424 --> 00:12:47,846
Today, we mostly shop alone,

362
00:12:47,846 --> 00:12:49,058
on our phones.

363
00:12:49,408 --> 00:12:51,704
If you were a teenager in nineteen

364
00:12:51,704 --> 00:12:53,460
eighty-four, you remember.

365
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:55,021
The Orange Julius.

366
00:12:55,201 --> 00:12:56,072
The arcade.

367
00:12:56,252 --> 00:12:57,343
The record store.

368
00:12:57,523 --> 00:12:59,648
The fountain full of pennies.

369
00:12:59,828 --> 00:13:02,198
Your friends, your feathered hair and your

370
00:13:02,198 --> 00:13:02,649
Walkman.

371
00:13:02,829 --> 00:13:04,443
The mall was your world,

372
00:13:04,443 --> 00:13:05,789
and for a few years,

373
00:13:05,789 --> 00:13:07,067
it was everybody's.

374
00:13:07,417 --> 00:13:09,158
So tell us in the comments.

375
00:13:09,338 --> 00:13:10,801
What was the name of your mall?

376
00:13:10,981 --> 00:13:12,805
What was your favorite store,

377
00:13:12,805 --> 00:13:14,693
and what did you always eat at

378
00:13:14,693 --> 00:13:15,637
the food court?

379
00:13:15,817 --> 00:13:16,997
Did you have a job at the

380
00:13:16,997 --> 00:13:17,233
mall?

381
00:13:17,413 --> 00:13:18,969
We read every comment.

382
00:13:19,149 --> 00:13:21,306
Next time, we're looking at what it

383
00:13:21,306 --> 00:13:24,141
meant to turn thirty in nineteen seventy-nine.

384
00:13:24,321 --> 00:13:26,211
Thank you for spending this time with

385
00:13:26,211 --> 00:13:26,364
me.
